{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>We are journeying, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say this — was the matter not already known to them? And our Sages of blessed memory sensed this and said (Sifrei Numbers 10:29) that in this statement the intention was to say that only one journey remained for them and they would arrive at the land of their desire. The verse may be further clarified by noting in the statement <b>ot I will give it to you</b>, that there was no need for him to say the word ״אותו״. However, it is because HaShem did not promise in His promises to our fathers to give us anything but the land of Canaan, and not the land of Sihon and Og, as their words of blessed memory state (Sifrei Deuteronomy, §299), and we have written about this above (Numbers 7:42), and since they were then journeying toward the land of Sihhon and Og, he said to Hobab: ״We are journeying to the place of which HaShem said, ot I will give it״ — and also ״to you״: even though He did not give it to our fathers, He will give it to us, for he knew through prophecy what was destined to be. And although they said in the Sifrei (ibid.), and this is their language: ״this excludes the land of Sihhon and Og, which you took on your own״ — end quote — nevertheless HaShem agreed and gave it to them." "Further, the statement <b>ot I will give it to you</b> intends — by using this designation — to allude that HaShem would give them the land of Sihhon and Og, and also to allude to Mosheh, for he would conquer it and bequeath it to the two and a half tribes. And he was precise in saying ״אותו״ to exclude the land of Canaan, for that He would not give to Mosheh but to Yehoshua." "<b>Come with us and we will do you good, etc.</b> The intent of these words is because our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei ch. 1, §132) that the land of Israel was apportioned to the tribes and not to converts; from this it follows there is no place to give a portion or inheritance to the children of Hobab within Israel except by way of a gift from their own portion. Therefore, when Mosheh came to make him a promise, he was precise in saying to him <b>and we will do you good</b> — meaning, from Israel's own portion they would give to him by way of a gift. And since Mosheh was concerned that they might not desire Mosheh's proposition on account of five concerns that could arise from the matter — three arising from the side of the gift, and two from the side of the promise — three from the side of the gift: first, that the recipient feels shame before the giver, along the lines of their saying (Yerushalmi Orlah 1:3) 'whoever eats what is not his own,' etc.; second, that the honor of the gift-recipient diminishes in the eyes of the giver, and he will no longer stand in his former esteem; third, that the smallness of the gift relative to a great man becomes a disgrace to him, and he will despise its existence because of its inadequacy. Two from the side of the promise: first, because there is room to fear that he may retract the good word he spoke to do, or out of miserliness when his eye turns against giving of his bounty, or because the love of the one making the promise toward him may diminish in his heart; and second, because his hand may not reach to fulfill it, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 26) on the verse ״the words of YHWH are pure words״ — therefore Mosheh acted wisely with the pleasantness of his words and removed all five of these concerns." "<b>And the opening</b> of his words illuminated in the statement <b>Come with us and we will do you good</b>, etc. — meaning: on account of your going with us we will do you good, and accordingly the benefit is not gratis and he can claim and recover it in court, and in this way he removed the concern of the shame of 'whoever eats what is not his own'; also the concern of the diminishment of his honor because he receives benefit from him; also the concern of nullification of the promise for he might retract — since after all he is not going with them and leaving his land and his inheritance except on this condition. And his saying <b>and we will do you good</b> here specifies the measure of the benefit — that the portion of the children of Hobab would be choice and the best of every portion of the people of Israel; they would give him the choicest portion, and so our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei Numbers 10:29) that they gave him the fertile land of Yericho." "And his saying <b>for HaShem has spoken good</b>, by this he nullified the two remaining concerns among those we listed, namely: the diminishment of the benefit and the inability to fulfill the promise — for HaShem has spoken good, and the goodness of HaShem is absolute, and to this measure the benefit will be of great scale and worthy of honor; also it is firm and secure and has no retraction, for HaShem is not a man, etc., that He should retract — and certainly not from His good word, for it is specifically regarding evil that He retracts, as it is written (Exodus 32:14) ״and HaShem relented concerning the evil״. And he was precise in saying ״concerning Israel״ for additional strengthening of the good, because it is for Israel and not for the nations of the world, and He will not relent concerning the good destined for them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:37.747107088Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}